### H2: The NYC Rental Market Has Rules Other Cities Don't

**Writing instruction:** This section introduces the three structural features of the NYC rental market that are unique and directly affect cost and search strategy: broker fees, rent stabilization, and no-fee inventory. Each must be defined in a single clear sentence when first introduced. This is both an expertise section and a definition-extraction target for AI engines. Cover all three. Keep each sub-section to 3-4 sentences max.

#### H3: Broker Fees

Define broker fee clearly. State the typical percentage (12-15% of annual rent). Give a concrete dollar example (a $3,000/month apartment = a broker fee of $4,320 to $5,400). Explain that no-fee apartments exist and should be a search filter.

#### H3: Rent Stabilization

Define rent stabilization in one sentence. State the approximate number of stabilized units in NYC (approximately 1 million as of 2024). Explain that pre-war buildings (built before 1974) in outer boroughs are more likely to have stabilized units. Explain the practical implication: annual rent increases are capped by law, making these units high-demand and fast-moving.

#### H3: No-Fee Apartments

Define no-fee apartment in one sentence. Explain that these are listed directly by landlords or through platforms that aggregate landlord-direct inventory. Frame this as a cost-saving filter worth applying at the start of any search.

